ed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:45bfcc64163ed4600e22fed3c4c6cf44d83e8f6be29d716ac6a70531316594c9","file":"preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","line":21,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"7aa26c9c15fa8e25542a5ef5a4406e8e00499ab75c34d1f0a5ec6ebc590ae540","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:45bfcc64163ed4600e22fed3c4c6cf44d83e8f6be29d716ac6a70531316594c9","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex#L21"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nEvery invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing\nof every finite order. More explicitly, under the assumptions above, for\nevery $k\\ge3$ and every $A_1,\\ldots,A_k\\in\\mathcal F$,\n\\[\n \\mu\\bigl(A_1\\cap T^{-n_1}A_2\\cap\\cdots\\cap\n             T^{-(n_1+\\cdots+n_{k-1})}A_k\\bigr)\n \\longrightarrow\\prod_{i=1}^k\\mu(A_i)\n\\]\nas $\\min(n_1,\\ldots,n_{k-1})\\to\\infty$ through positive integers.\nNo standardness or countable-generation assumption is imposed on the\noriginal probability space."}
